<%# The slide a buyer uses to judge how much they're getting before reading a
    word of the description.

    "Also includes a low-ink version" asserted in a bullet is a claim; one page
    shown printed pale beside the colour grid is proof. That proof used to be a
    whole second slide — the identical grid rendered again with hide_colors —
    which cost up to eight extra Grover renders and spent a slot in a gallery
    now capped at exactly ten. One inset card makes the same point.

    Names the boards rather than quoting a page count: for a set, "People,
    Feelings, Food, Play" is most of the reason to buy, and a page number tells
    a buyer nothing. The grid is capped and shaped by ContentTilePlan — an
    uncapped row of 20 pages rendered hairlines nobody could read. %>
<div class="slide bg-sky">
  <% if @logo %>
    <img class="logo-corner" src="<%= @logo %>" alt="SpeakAnyWay" />
  <% end %>

  <div class="slide-stack">
    <div class="title-banner"><%= Printables::SlideCopy.whats_included_title %></div>

    <%# Tile captions only earn their space when there is more than one board to
        tell apart — on a single-board printable the label just repeats the
        title two inches above it. %>
    <% show_labels = @tiles.size > 1 %>

    <div class="included-stage stretch" style="--tile-max: <%= @tile_max_px %>px;">
      <%# Both tracks are minmax(0, 1fr), and the 0 matters as much as the 1fr.
          A bare `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, so a track can never shrink below
          its content's min-content width — and a tile caption is `nowrap`, so a
          long board name silently pushed the last column off the slide. The
          rows are explicit for the other half of the same problem: an auto row
          gives the page card nothing definite to size itself against. %>
      <div class="tile-grid"
           style="grid-template-columns: repeat(<%= @columns %>, minmax(0, 1fr));
                  grid-template-rows: repeat(<%= @rows %>, minmax(0, 1fr));">
        <% @tiles.each do |tile| %>
          <div class="tile">
            <div class="paper">
              <img src="<%= tile[:data_uri] %>" alt="<%= tile[:label] %>" />
            </div>
            <% if show_labels && tile[:label].present? %>
              <div class="tile-label"><%= tile[:label] %></div>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <% if @overflow_note.present? %>
        <div class="overflow-note"><%= @overflow_note %></div>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="included-footer">
      <div class="included-panel">
        <div class="included-title">In your download</div>
        <ul class="bullets on-dark">
          <% @items.each do |item| %>
            <li><%= item %></li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <%# The aspect is written inline from the render's REAL dimensions, never
          left to `height: auto` inside a flex item — that percentage resolves
          against an indefinite height, is ignored, and silently slices the
          bottom off the page. Same rule as the grid's cards. %>
      <% if @low_ink_proof %>
        <div class="low-ink-proof">
          <div class="paper" style="aspect-ratio: <%= @low_ink_proof[:width] %> / <%= @low_ink_proof[:height] %>;">
            <img src="<%= @low_ink_proof[:data_uri] %>" alt="<%= Printables::SlideCopy.low_ink_headline %>" />
          </div>
          <div class="proof-label"><%= Printables::SlideCopy.low_ink_headline %></div>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
